From cb2b84bb921d2ee24164e259dc87978c13522bf3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Baron <dbaron@chromium.org>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 15:33:17 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] [Backport] CVE-2026-76031: Use after free in Input (1/2)

Manual cherry-pick of patch originally reviewed on
https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/8611718:
Don't send a cross-origin relatedTarget on a PointerEvent boundary event, and add more general defense-in-depth for relatedTarget.

This prevents exposing a Node from another origin as the relatedTarget
on pointerout, pointerover, pointerleave, and pointerenter events.

The relatedTarget is instead null, except that when the two documents
have an inner/outer relationship in the frame hierarchy, the events in
the outer document use the frame owner element (e.g., iframe) in that
document that contains the inner document as the relatedTarget.

Same-origin cross-document boundary events retain the existing behavior
of exposing the related target Node across documents, which is tested in
a tentative WPT.  It's possible that we should also change this
behavior, though, depending on how other engines behave.

This also includes a second defense-in-depth change to check
relatedTarget for all event types (MouseEvent, PointerEvent, and
FocusEvent) that report it to script (not just this particular
dispatching path) and set it to null if it is a cross-origin node.

Both of these behavior changes are guarded by the
`ClearCrossOriginRelatedTarget` runtime-enabled feature (status:
stable).

As a followup (to roll out via the normal release branch and not be
merged to older branches) I intend to change the cross-origin check to
be just a cross-document check, which will match the behavior of other
browser engines.  This two-stage change will reduce the compatibility
risk of the change that needs merging.

